#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116351} :activity/id #uuid "cf42fdc1-dee5-53a6-abe0-79ec4ef7b900" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048420 :topic/id #uuid "9d6972e8-70d1-541f-8fd7-7896e8369046" :topic/math-academy-id 1691} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116351 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116507 :tutorial/content "A function $f(x)$ is said to be **differentiable** at a point $x=a$ if the derivative $f'(a)$ exists.\n\nIn general:\n\n- If a function is not continuous at some point, then it is not differentiable at that point either.\n\n- If a function is differentiable at some point, then it must be continuous at that point.\n\nFor example, suppose we are given a step function $y=f(x)$ that has a jump discontinuity at $x=3,$ given below. We will verify that $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=3$ by showing that $f'(3)$ does not exist. \n\n![](images/9f/9f91b034a27ce7fd2b432f0b094e4b40098247916b78f9c1b3eb64c7214f69b4.png)\n\nThe value $f'(3)$ is a limit, so to check whether it exists, we need to compute the left and right-sided limits.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(3)={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{f(x)-f(3)}{x-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $x$ approaches $3$ from the left, we have that $f(x) = 2.$ So the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}\\frac{f(x)-f(3)}{x-3} & ={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}\\frac{2-2}{x-3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, as $x$ approaches $3$ from the right, we have that $f(x)=1.$ So the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}\\frac{f(x)-f(3)}{x-3} & ={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}\\frac{1-2}{x-3}={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}\\frac{-1}{x-3}=-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the left and right-sided limits are not equal, the overall limit does not exist. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(3)={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{f(x)-f(3)}{x-3}=\\text{DNE}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=3.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "60845968-f9d5-58f2-9205-7c60bc949ed6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 332 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "62b549c4-9fa6-595c-9705-edecd57fab60" :step/math-academy-id 615 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116354}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116352 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115380 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114920 :question/id #uuid "511342eb-691a-57be-a7cc-f10ab430b575" :question/math-academy-id "e-131"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "794398c0-ffbf-5971-a411-f236607f56b8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048904} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050574}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115887} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115888} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237859} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237862} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332844} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341927} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341928} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341929}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function is Differentiable at a Point Given a Graph"} :step/id #uuid "7d001457-3a19-5fb6-b582-e4447e917a71" :step/math-academy-id 616 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116355}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116353 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115381 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114921 :question/id #uuid "68eb6b1f-955c-550b-9232-3c6115ac220f" :question/math-academy-id "e-132"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b4100f8-8a66-5243-b0b6-c36b9d17dac3" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051076}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115882} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115886} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237869} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237872} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237873} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329826} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332845} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341932} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341933} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341934} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392595}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Which Functions are Differentiable at a Given Point From the Function Expressions"} :step/id #uuid "d3a114b6-40b8-5189-a0c2-30250448ba57" :step/math-academy-id 619} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116354 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116514 :tutorial/content "If a function is continuous, that does *not* mean that it's differentiable!\n\nTo demonstrate, let's consider the function $f(x)=|x|.$ The function $f(x)$ is continuous everywhere, including the point $x=0.$ But is $f(x)$ also differentiable everywhere? \n\n![](images/18/18644551713e0c1d451cf27bd43f3cc8fc8d7064d7af79df3cd2070af782c25e.png)\n\nFor $x < 0,$ the graph consists of a downwards-sloping line with slope $-1.$ For $x > 0,$ the graph consists of an upward-sloping line with slope $1.$ So, the derivative of the absolute value function is given by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-1\\, & x<0, \\\\ 1\\, & x>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the derivative of $f(x)=|x|$ does not exist at $x=0.$ This is because the limit of the slope as we approach $x=0$ from the left is not equal to the limit as we approach from the right.\n\nIn general, a continuous function $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=a$ if the function $f(x)$ has any of the following features:\n\n- A **corner point** at $x=a.$ For example, $f(x)=|x|$ at $x=0.$\n\n- A **cusp** at $x=a.$ For example, $f(x) = \\sqrt{|x|}$ at $x=0.$\n\n- A **vertical tangent** at $x=a.$ For example, $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}$ at $x=0.$\n\n![](images/28/28a6f3a424556e1e60e4959b03a5c38a1ff6a5d0efb110a0e5a4bfadc7072511.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "0df8723a-c6d6-562d-8cde-b44a47dd70bc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 335 :tutorial/title "Continuity Does Not Imply Differentiability"} :step/id #uuid "872a7f94-c8a5-5f55-a91f-39909aca384f" :step/math-academy-id 622 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116352}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116355 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115382 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114922 :question/id #uuid "de600cc4-19fc-53a7-babc-fc387eff718c" :question/math-academy-id "e-133"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7dcbd56f-0fc2-59c5-a016-4272be4734c7" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115883} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115885} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237864} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237865} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237866} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237867} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237868} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329823} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329824} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329825} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329827} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329828} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332843} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341931}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Points at Which a Function is Not Differentiable"} :step/id #uuid "4284ae9c-1207-5fcc-829e-569528302322" :step/math-academy-id 623 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116353}}] :activity/title "Continuity and Differentiability of Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186115458}]}
